hitecture community of friends that i  went to school with  most of my friends here in vancouver  were not in architecture  and because none of them had to do a  master's they'd already kind of been  working in their careers for a couple of  years  and  yes uh comparison is the thief of joy  but i  kind of started to feel really  behind in life so  that  kind of  lit a fire under my ass now  after actually getting licensed i am  seeing a lot more tangible benefits to  becoming licensed  like  an increase in salary and my overall  market value as an architect has  increased significantly  but i'm gonna be talking about all of  that in another video so there were  some  um i guess you could call them  strategies  to how i approached my licensure  and i've  kind of broken them down into six points  here  be selective where you intern so i  started working the summer after i  graduated from my master's  and the job search was quite tough  um i thought i had quite a good  portfolio and i had won quite a few  architectural awards but i was still  getting very few responses from firms so  when i finally did get an offer  i actually got two different offers from  two different firms around the same time  when that happened i was totally over  the moon  but  basically uh before i started  uh job searching i kind of asked a lot  of my older friends for some advice on  like how to pick the right firm and like  the whole  internship process  and a lot of them advised me to  try to look for a firm that could help  me get licensed as fast as possible  because apparently a lot of interns they  have to switch firms because  the firm that they were at they won't  put them on projects or they didn't have  projects that were in phases that they  had to get hours in  um  so  when you graduate it's very easy to  take the first job offer that you get  and  you  might think like oh i can stay here for  a little while  and move if it's not the right fit or if  i'm not getting the hours but actually  like if you're already working somewhere  it kind of takes a lot of effort to move  somewhere else  and  even if you do that process is going to  take months  so  now you've already wasted months of your  precious internship hours so  try to be pretty selective about it  right from the get-go  and ask a lot of questions during your  interview process  be direct  um  ask about how the firm helps interns  collect their hours  what kind of projects am i going to be  working on do you have like resources  to help me with the exam process and  yeah try to ask those questions even if  it's a little bit awkward  at least that way you've kind of done  your due diligence and you've done your  best to try to figure out what it's like  try to figure out what type of projects  you're going to be working on  [Music]  so the way your hours works is you can  use any project in your office to  collect hours in different categories  like they don't all have to be from one  project  so if you have a  new house that's in schematic design  phase you can use that project to  collect your  site analysis hours and like schematic  design hours  and if you have a project in ca um in  construction min then you can collect uh  ca hours from that particular project  the only problem with that is that  you're really getting like bits and  pieces of information and i think the  best learning experience is actually  if you see a project through  from start to finish  and so therefore i think a small project  will be much better for you like a house  or like a retrofit  because they have shorter time spans and  you can actually see the project through  from like beginning to schematic design  to construction to project closeout in  my office  my project was a small office renault  for the city of coquitlam  and i was a little bit concerned  actually because it's not a new building  so there wasn't any like site work or  like foundations that had to go in  but  what's more  important is 