e is like a facade  so  um for me every time i have some kind of  indication of what the people are gonna  be like  and you know good people are pretty hard  to find  i do try to make a conscious effort to  go after that and this is actually still  one of the main factors  for me in choosing where i work  log your hours diligently and check in  with your project architect regularly so  i tracked my hours every single day for  everything that i did um in our time  tracking app  and i just added a hashtag for the  internship hours category so  let's say i was detailing a roof for a  house i would log those hours and add  the hashtag constructiondocuments  so when i have to submit my hours i can  just search up construction documents  and  it'll show me exactly how many hours i  have in that category  and  it's also going to tell me which project  it's in i also created an excel sheet  that adds all of the hours and kind of  tells me how many hours i have left in  each category  so if you want a copy of that  i'll leave the link below and you can  download it and use it as well the  category i had the hardest time filling  was the contract negotiations  which is  basically reviewing bids and rfis and  i had completed all of my hours and it  was just  that category that i was missing  and  our office didn't have any projects  going into tender at that time  so i talked to the principal and we kind  of came up with a creative solution  basically i helped them create a  checklist for um tenders  so like i read through all the questions  from contractors that came in during an  rfi process  and i kind of made a list of things that  we need to be sure to include in our  drawings or in documents  i also reviewed all of the bids and kind  of try to record any red flags that  we could anticipate for other projects  write your exams as fast as possible i  know when you finish school i'm sure the  last thing you want to do is write  another exam but  i think it's better to just 